#!/usr/bin/env python3
r"""herdr over mosh: no opaque background fill, and real images (README.md).

    herdr-patch <host> [herdr arguments...]               on your computer
    herdr-patch [--channel TOKEN] [herdr arguments...]    on the server: the mosh command
    herdr-patch --read TOKEN                              on the server: the image reader
    herdr-patch --help

The first argument decides where it runs. If it does not start with "-", it is
the host, and herdr-patch runs on your computer. With no argument, or with a
first argument that starts with "-", herdr-patch runs on the server. Herdr
arguments default to --session default.

On the server, herdr-patch runs herdr in its own pseudo-terminal. Keys and
window size go through unchanged. Herdr 0.9.3 keeps a "host background" color
in the server and, after a client attaches again, paints it over older panes
as an opaque fill (herdrdev/herdr#3773). mosh never answers the OSC 10/11 color
queries, so the fill shows as a solid block instead of the terminal's own,
often transparent, background. In herdr's output, a truecolor background
(48;2;R;G;B, 48:2:R:G:B, 48:2::R:G:B or 48:2:<cs>:R:G:B) whose RGB is on the
fill list becomes the default background (49). Exits with herdr's exit status.

Images: herdr sends each pane image as kitty graphics (a transmit, then one
placement per cell row after a CUP, deletes on scroll and clear). mosh drops
them, so the server takes them out of the stream and paints each placement as
half-block cells, which every mosh client can show. Each half cell is the mean
of at most 4 sampled pixels, so the work for a placement does not grow with the
size of the image. With --channel, it also serves the graphics on a unix socket
that only this user can open, and `herdr-patch --read TOKEN` copies them to
stdout.

What the server changes in herdr's output, and nothing else: a fill-list
background becomes SGR 49; kitty graphics commands (ESC _ G ... ESC \) are
taken out; half-block cells are painted into the cells of each image (and
blanked when herdr deletes the image). Every other byte goes through unchanged.

On your computer, `herdr-patch <host>` starts `mosh <host> -- herdr-patch
--channel TOKEN ...` in a pseudo-terminal, with a new random TOKEN, and reads
the graphics of exactly that server through `ssh <host> herdr-patch --read
TOKEN`. Keys and window size go through to mosh unchanged. The graphics go into
the terminal output only between escape sequences, wrapped in ESC 7 ... ESC 8.
After mosh output that can erase image cells (EL, ECH, ED, scrolls, insert and
delete), and after a resize, the current placements go out again. When the ssh
channel stops, mosh goes on without images and the channel connects again.

HERDR_PATCH_FILL: comma-separated hex colors, default "1e1e1e,232136" (herdr's
own fallback and a rose-pine-moon background). An empty value changes nothing.
HERDR_BIN_PATH: the herdr program the server runs, default "herdr".

Needs only the Python 3.9 standard library (the one macOS ships). Your computer
needs mosh, ssh, and a terminal with kitty graphics, such as WezTerm or kitty.
"""

